    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "You need a new roof in Utah if your asphalt shingle roof is 20 to 30 years old, or if you notice system-wide failures like severe sagging, widespread missing or curling shingles, and active indoor water leaks.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "if your asphalt shingle roof is 20 to 30 years old, or if you notice system-wide failures like severe sagging, widespread missing or curling shingles, and active indoor water leaks"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Warning Signs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Age (20+ Years): Standard asphalt roofs in Utah\u2019s climate last about 20 to 30 years. If your roof is approaching or past this mark, normal wear means replacement is usually wiser than repeated patching.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Curling, Buckling, or Cracking Shingles: Shingles that cup upward, crack, or lose flexibility can no longer seal out Utah's wind-driven rain and snowmelt.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Missing or Blown-Off Shingles: Severe windstorms or heavy snow can tear shingles away. If you see bare spots or exposed roof decking from the ground, the system is compromised.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Excessive Granule Loss: Check your gutters and downspouts. If they are filled with thick layers of dark, sandy granules, your shingles have lost their protective UV coating and are breaking down.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Sagging Roof Decking: A droopy or spongy roofline\u2014especially along the ridges or low-slope areas\u2014often points to structural wood rot caused by heavy snow loads or chronic moisture.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Water Stains and Attic Leaks: Dark ceiling stains, damp attic insulation, or light peeking through your attic boards mean water is actively penetrating the roof deck.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Brittle or Failing Flashing: Rusted or separated metal flashing around chimneys, vent pipes, and valleys lets water pour into vulnerable seams.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Next Steps and Local Resources"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Get an Independent Evaluation: Schedule a detailed assessment with a trusted local evaluator like Roofing Utah to get photo documentation of your roof's actual condition rather than relying on a door-to-door sales pitch.",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Roofing Utah",
                "link": "https://roofingutah.com/roof-repair/roof-inspection/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Understand Local Costs: Full asphalt shingle roof replacements in Utah typically range from $8,500 to $18,000, scaling higher for larger homes or specialty materials, as outlined in the Homer Roofing Learning Center .",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "text": "Homer Roofing Learning Center",
                "link": "https://homerroofing.net/learning-center/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Check Permitting Rules: Most Utah municipalities require contractors to pull a formal building permit before tearing off and replacing a roof.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How old your roof is right now"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "What specific damage you are seeing (like leaks or missing shingles)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Your general area in Utah"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you figure out what questions to ask a local contractor or what to check next."
      }
    ],
